<Analysis>
<![CDATA[ (9/16) The Rockies lost 12 of 14 to close out the month of August and have lost 9 of 13 in September thus far. It's no secret that saves have been hard to come by. Fortunately, when a save chance arises, there's a strong likelihood that Jordan Romano will get the call - even if he's blown 2 of his last 4 opportunities. Romano has actually recorded Colorado's last 9 saves dating back to mid-July. Prior to Romano's arrival in Colorado, manager Warren Schaeffer deployed a closer-by-committee. He opened 2026 using 5 different relievers for saves through the first few weeks of the season - Jimmy Herget, Victor Vodnik, Juan Mejia, Antonio Senzatela and Zach Agnos. Vodnik went on to record 3 of the team's next 5 saves through the end of April, but he was subsequently traded to Miami. Colorado stumbled in May, posting a mere 8-20 record during which there simply weren't many save chances available. Mejia actually led the Rockies with 2 of the team's 3 saves in May. In June, it was Herget and Jaden Hill recording 2 saves apiece to lead the team. When the calendar flipped to July is when Romano resurfaced. While he's been successful closing out games for Colorado thus far, it's still pretty difficult to trust his stability given his inconsistent performance in recent campaigns. ]]>
</Analysis>
